--- machine\system\time
canonical: Machine\System\Time

The machine's time policy: where the clock comes from and what it is
allowed to believe.

timed reads this subtree, polls the sources it names, and keeps the
clock. It holds `SeSystemtimePrivilege` and nothing else; the grant is a
record on its own service SID under `Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy`, so no
other service running as LocalService acquires it. Administrators hold
the same privilege by default, but a clock set behind timed's back is one
it measures as an error: put right at the next poll, or, if it is more
than 1000 seconds out after timed has been synchronised, refused as a
panic. To set the clock by hand, set `Automatic` to 0 and ask timed
(`clock set`, or System Settings).

Everything here takes effect within a second or two of being written —
timed watches this subtree — except that a change to `Servers` restarts
the sources it names, which costs a few minutes of accuracy while they
converge.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read). That descriptor is the only gate on
changing where this machine gets its time. `clock` reads over timed's
socket and writes nothing, so there is deliberately no second permission
model: `reg set` is how the policy is changed.

--- machine\system\time servers
canonical: Machine\System\Time Servers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent — the shipped fallback set is used)
applies: live

The time sources to use, highest precedence. Each entry is a host,
optionally with a port, optionally followed by option words:

    time.example.org
    time.example.org:4460
    dc01.corp.example prefer
    ntp.lan unauthenticated

- `prefer` — lead with this one when several agree. It breaks ties and
  nothing more: a preferred source that disagrees with the majority is
  still discarded, because a preference is a statement about which server
  to lean on, not a licence to be wrong.
- `unauthenticated` (or `noauth`) — this source speaks plain NTP rather
  than NTS. Only honoured when `AllowUnauthenticated` is `1`; otherwise
  the source is refused and logged, so that turning the machine-wide
  switch off actually turns everything off rather than leaving per-source
  exceptions behind.

A misspelled option is an error and the entry is ignored with a warning. A
silently accepted typo would be a configuration that reads correctly and
is not.

**Precedence is first-match, not merge.** If this value names anything, it
is the whole source list; DHCP and the fallback set are not added to it. A
machine told exactly which servers to use must not also be quietly talking
to somebody else's.

When this value is absent, timed uses `0.time.peios.org` through
`3.time.peios.org` — CNAMEs to three independent operators (Cloudflare,
Netnod, PTB) in three jurisdictions. Three is the minimum at which one
liar can be outvoted, and independence is what makes the votes worth
counting: three servers run by one operator would agree with each other
about anything.

**Use at least three.** With one source there is nothing to check it
against and it is believed. With two that disagree there is no way to tell
which is right, and timed reports itself unsynchronised rather than
guessing.

--- machine\system\time allowunauthenticated
canonical: Machine\System\Time AllowUnauthenticated
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Whether a source may be used without NTS.

- `0` (the default) — every source must authenticate. NTS gives each
  packet an AEAD tag over the whole packet, computed with a key
  established over an authenticated TLS session, so forging a reply
  requires the key.
- `1` — sources marked `unauthenticated` in `Servers` may be used. Their
  only defence is that the server must echo the exact 64 bits timed put in
  its request, which timed fills with a random number for this reason. An
  attacker who cannot see the request cannot forge a reply; one who *can*
  see it can forge freely.

The default is `0` because the alternative outcome is worse than it looks.
On a network that blocks port 4460, `0` gives a machine that reports
itself unsynchronised — which is honest, and which the build-time floor
keeps usable, because the clock is still recent enough for TLS to work.
`1` on that network gives a machine synchronised to whoever answered on
port 123.

Time is what certificate expiry, Kerberos tickets and log ordering all
rest on, so an attacker who can move it has more than a wrong clock.

--- machine\system\time usefromdhcp
canonical: Machine\System\Time UseFromDHCP
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Whether to use the time servers a DHCP lease offers (option 42).

- `0` (the default) — ignored. netd still reports them, and `clock
  sources` will not list them.
- `1` — used, when `Servers` is absent. They are polled without NTS, since
  a DHCP-advertised server is not going to be running NTS-KE.

Off by default, as on Windows, and for the same reason: on a network you
do not control, the DHCP server's idea of the time is the attacker's idea
of the time — and setting this to `1` is a statement that you trust every
network this machine will ever join.

Reasonable to turn on for a machine that only ever attaches to a network
you run, where the DHCP server and the time server are the same
appliance.

--- machine\system\time minpoll
canonical: Machine\System\Time MinPoll
type: REG_DWORD
default: 6
applies: live

The shortest interval between polls of one source, as a power of two
seconds: `6` is 64 seconds, `10` is about 17 minutes.

Clamped to the range 4 (16 seconds) to 17. The floor is not negotiable: a
configuration error must not be able to turn this machine into a nuisance
at somebody else's public server.

Lowering it makes the clock converge faster after a disturbance and costs
the servers more traffic. There is rarely a reason to: a long interval
measures *frequency* better than a short one measures phase, and it is the
frequency estimate that holds the clock right between polls.

--- machine\system\time maxpoll
canonical: Machine\System\Time MaxPoll
type: REG_DWORD
default: 10
applies: live

The longest interval between polls, as a power of two seconds. timed
lengthens the interval towards this while the clock is being held steadily
and shortens it when the offset starts moving.

Clamped to the same range as `MinPoll`, and raised to `MinPoll` if it is
set below it. Past about 17 minutes a typical crystal drifts further
between polls than the frequency estimate can predict, so raising it
trades accuracy for a traffic saving that is already negligible.

--- machine\system\time controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Time ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: (absent — the compiled default is used)
applies: live

A security descriptor for timed's control object, governing what a caller
may do over `/run/timed/time.sock`.

Two rights: `0x1` query and `0x2` control. The compiled default gives
SYSTEM and Administrators both, and Everyone query — reading is open
because what time the machine thinks it is, and how well it knows, is not
a secret, and a program deciding whether the clock is trustworthy enough
to validate a certificate should not need a privilege to find out.

Only `reload` and `set` need control. Changing the time policy itself is a
registry write governed by this key's own descriptor, not by this value.

--- machine\system\time automatic
canonical: Machine\System\Time Automatic
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

Whether timed keeps the clock from its sources.

- `1` (the default) — timed polls the sources and steers the clock.
  Asking it to set the clock is refused, because the next poll would put
  it back.
- `0` — timed polls nothing, and the clock is set by hand: `clock set`,
  or System Settings, asks timed to set it, which needs the control right
  on `ControlSecurity`. The frequency timed last learned stays in force,
  so the clock drifts no faster than the crystal allows. A time set this
  way reaches the hardware clock within eleven minutes, so it survives a
  reboot.

`clock status` says `set by hand` while this is 0, and the state is
`unsynchronised`: nothing is checking the clock against anything, and a
service that needs `timed:synchronised` waits.

Setting it back to 1 starts the sources afresh. However far the clock was
set from the truth, the first agreement steps it there, as at startup.

--- machine\system\time timezone
canonical: Machine\System\Time TimeZone
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — UTC)
valid: a zone name from the tzdata package, such as `Europe/London` or `America/New_York`
applies: live

The machine's time zone. Everything that shows a local time follows it:
the C library, which reads `/etc/localtime`, and so every program; the
desktop's clock; and peinit's calendar timers with no zone of their own.

timed keeps `/etc/localtime` a copy of the named file under
`/usr/share/zoneinfo`, made again whenever this value changes and every
time timed starts, so a tzdata upgrade reaches it by the next boot.
Programs already running pick the new zone up the next time they look,
which for most is the next time they show a time.

A name tzdata does not have is refused and logged, and the machine stays
on the zone it was on; `clock status` shows the zone in force, across a
restart of timed too. A refused name is tried again whenever a value
under `Machine\System\Time` changes and every ten minutes, so a zone a tzdata upgrade adds is taken
up without the value being written again. Absent is UTC.

There is one zone for the whole machine. Times are stored and exchanged
in UTC throughout Peios; this is only how they are shown.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\s-1-5-80-2061758774-2413367500-3090050229-3167896262-4120155161 privileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\S-1-5-80-2061758774-2413367500-3090050229-3167896262-4120155161 Privileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: SeSystemtimePrivilege, SeChangeNotifyPrivilege, SeAuditPrivilege
valid: privilege names; see the generic page for the vocabulary
applies: next start

Where timed's authority to set the clock actually comes from.

That SID is timed's, derived — it is the SHA-1 of `TIMED` as UTF-16LE,
split into five little-endian words after `S-1-5-80`. It is written out in
full rather than abbreviated so that looking the key up actually finds
this page instead of authd's generic one.

authd unions the privileges of every SID on a token, and a service token
carries the `S-1-5-80` SID derived from the service name. A record named
for timed's service SID therefore grants the privilege to timed alone —
not to LocalService, which trustd and resolvd also run as.

`SeSystemtimePrivilege` is what KACS maps to `CAP_SYS_TIME`, which is what
`adjtimex(2)` checks. `SeChangeNotifyPrivilege` is not decoration: peinit
*removes* every privilege outside a service's `RequiredPrivileges` list,
and without it a process cannot traverse a directory to reach a file — so
naming only the first would produce a daemon that can set the clock and
cannot open its own drift file. `SeAuditPrivilege` lets timed write a
`timed.clock.stepped` event each time it steps the clock; without it the
clock still moves, the event is not written, and timed logs a warning
instead.

Removing this record leaves timed running and measuring, and unable to
act: `adjtimex` fails with `EPERM` and the clock is never steered. That is
a legitimate configuration for a machine whose time comes from a
hypervisor, but `clock status` will report the offset growing, so prefer
disabling the service.
